17918	{"artifactId":"DEF-e6ddcdcb3d9cf330","entry":{"id":"review-7ac90eb5a5736f2efee2d10781799f9d","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-30T18:43:16.510Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"define","stage":"define","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"cl1","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"[[c3]] cites docs/epics/review-planned-changes-through-agent-investigation-E20261028c32aa605/ROLLBACK.md as containing the quoted text 'prior approvals do not authorize restarting the abandoned implementation.'","evidence":"grep for the exact phrase 'prior approvals do not authorize restarting the abandoned implementation' returns 1 match in docs/epics/review-planned-changes-through-agent-investigation-E20261028c32aa605/ROLLBACK.md:9, which reads in full: '...This rollback supersedes implementation/activation instructions in the retained epic and prerequisite documents; prior approvals do not authorize restarting the abandoned implementation.' This confirms the cited file does contain the quoted text verbatim. The read evidence error (out-of-range 1-40) is a tooling limitation, not proof against the citation — the grep match already confirms the premise.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"cl2","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"[[c4]] cites AGENTS.md as the source of the convention 'No direct cloud REST calls from our process.'","evidence":"grep for the exact convention text returns exactly one match: AGENTS.md:12 — \"**No direct cloud REST calls from our process.** Cloud LLM access happens through the locally-installed `claude` and `codex` CLI binaries (via `CliProvider`). Auth + quota stay with the user's CLI OAuth sessions. Direct REST providers to Anthropic / OpenAI / Gemini / Mistral must not be reintroduced.\" This confirms AGENTS.md is indeed the source location of the cited convention, matching the premise exactly.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"cl3","kind":"inventory","severity":"MED","premise":"The epic defines exactly three stories: s1 (size L), s2 (size M), s3 (size M), numbered 6.1-6.3.","evidence":"grep for /^### 6\\.\\d+ STORY-/ in DEF.md returns exactly 3 matches, confirming the count, numbering and IDs: line 69 '### 6.1 STORY-e6ddcdcb3d9cf330-s1 — Get a useful background review of submitted committed work', line 86 '### 6.2 STORY-e6ddcdcb3d9cf330-s2 — Recover interruptions and re-review fixes using the same review history', line 101 '### 6.3 STORY-e6ddcdcb3d9cf330-s3 — Run independent reviews concurrently and clean up after story approval'. This confirms the story count (3), the numbering (6.1-6.3) and the s1/s2/s3 ids/anchors. However, the gathered evidence contains no size-field data (no 'Size:' or similar line was captured for any of the three stories), so the specific size claims in the premise (s1=L, s2=M, s3=M) are not addressed by the evidence at all and cannot be confirmed or refuted.","action":"Re-run the grep/read against each story's size field (e.g. a 'Size:' or 'Estimate:' line under each ### 6.x heading) to confirm s1=L, s2=M, s3=M before relying on the size breakdown; if confirmed no edit is needed, if not, correct the sizes in the premise/artifact text.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The count/numbering/IDs are verified sound, but the size labels are an unverified sub-claim requiring an additional targeted probe (grep for the size field per story) rather than a mechanical text fix, since there is no evidence yet to derive a replacement value from.","options":["Re-run probes capturing each story's size field and confirm/correct s1/s2/s3 sizes","Treat the size claim as advisory since the core inventory (3 stories, 6.1-6.3, correct ids) is verified","Have the artifact author re-cite the size fields with file:line so they are machine-checkable"]}},{"claimId":"cl4","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"The Constraints table (section 5) lists exactly eight constraints, k1 through k8.","evidence":"grep of `^\\| `k\\d` \\|` against docs/epics/review-committed-work-task-checkpoints-E20261030e6ddcdcb/DEF.md returns exactly 8 rows (lines 58-65), one each for k1 through k8 — matching the anchors listed (k1, k2, k3, k4, k5, k6, k7, k8) with no gaps or duplicates.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"cl5","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"The References section (section 7) lists exactly four references, c1 through c4, and every [[cN]] token used in the body (k1-k8, ac's, non-goals, assumptions) resolves to one of these four.","evidence":"The bold-anchor grep (\\*\\*\\[\\[c\\d\\]\\]\\*\\*) finds exactly 4 matches, at DEF.md:118-121, defining c1, c2, c3, c4 in the References section. The broader grep (\\[\\[c\\d\\]\\]) finds 14 total matches, and every body usage outside the References block — line 52 ([[c2]]), line 58 k1 ([[c1]]), line 59 k2 ([[c1]]), line 60 k3 ([[c1]]), line 61 k4 ([[c1]]), line 62 k5 ([[c1]]), line 63 k6 ([[c1]]), line 64 k7 ([[c3]]), line 65 k8 ([[c4]]), and line 75 Extends ([[c2]]) — references only c1, c2, c3, or c4. No c5+ or other undefined token appears anywhere in the matches.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"cl6","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"Task and attempt statuses form a closed set of exactly six values: pending, running, passed, changes_requested, incomplete, failed (k5), and every other status mention in the document (e.g. s2 ac1/ac3's 'failed or incomplete', ac5's 'failed then incomplete then changes_requested then passed') uses only members of this set.","evidence":"DEF.md:62 (k5) states verbatim: 'Task and attempt statuses are pending, running, passed, changes_requested, incomplete, failed' — exactly the six values the premise names. ac5 (line 83) uses 'failed then incomplete then changes_requested then passed' — all four terms are members of the k5 set. ac2 (line 97) and ac3 (line 98) both use 'failed or incomplete' — both members of the set. No evidence of any status term outside the six-value set appearing anywhere in the document.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"cl7","kind":"ordering","severity":"LOW","premise":"The terminal aggregate precedence order — failed, then incomplete, then changes_requested, then passed — is stated identically in k5 and restated consistently in s1/ac5 without contradiction elsewhere in the document.","evidence":"DEF.md:62 (k5) states: 'terminal aggregate precedence is failed, incomplete, changes_requested, passed.' DEF.md:83 (ac5) states: 'aggregate precedence is failed then incomplete then changes_requested then passed.' Both enumerate the identical four-element order (failed, incomplete, changes_requested, passed) with no divergence in membership or sequence, and no other grep match surfaces a conflicting ordering elsewhere in the document.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"cl8","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"[[c2]] asserts that an existing code-review runner, task contracts, request handler and review-session providers are present in the codebase as reuse candidates for this epic.","evidence":"Of the four reuse candidates [[c2]] claims exist, only 'review-session providers' is solidly grounded: CliProvider.runReviewSession and an optional LLMProvider.runReviewSession implemented by OllamaProvider are cited throughout docs/epics/.../S001/LLD.md (lines 237, 279-291, 533) and corroborated by src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:90 (ReviewSessionTimeoutError). 'Code-review runner' has partial support -- LLD.md:714 references existing entry points runCodeReview/runReview/runTemplateReview as pre-build code -- but the class-shaped grep for class Review(Runner|Session) found only an error class, not a runner class. 'Task contracts' returned 0 matches for the literal string TaskContract, and 'request handler' returned only 2 irrelevant hits (a review markdown doc and an unrelated JetBrains FUS event-schema field hasPreRequestHandler) -- neither addresses a code-review request handler. These two zero/irrelevant results are narrow, literal-CamelCase searches that may not match this codebase's actual naming (e.g. a generic Task type in src/workflow/, or an IPC/daemon handler under a different name), not proof the constructs are absent.","action":"Re-run grounding with the actual symbol names intended for 'task contracts' and 'request handler' (search src/workflow/ for Task-shaped types, and src/daemon/ for IPC/request-handling constructs), and have the author cite concrete file:line evidence for those two reuse candidates in [[c2]], or narrow the premise to only the two candidates (code-review runner, review-session providers) the evidence actually supports.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The gap is an evidentiary/terminology mismatch, not a confirmed absence -- a human should either supply correct symbol names so the premise can be re-verified, or accept a narrowed premise.","options":["Narrow [[c2]] to only 'code-review runner' and 'review-session providers', dropping the unverified 'task contracts' / 'request handler' claims","Re-ground [[c2]] with corrected search terms (e.g. Task type under src/workflow/, handler registration under src/daemon/) and cite the resulting file:line hits","Leave [[c2]] as-is but mark 'task contracts' and 'request handler' as could-not-verify pending author clarification"]}},{"claimId":"cl9","kind":"ordering","severity":"LOW","premise":"Story dependencies are strictly ordered: s2 depends on s1, and s3 depends on both s1 and s2, with no cyclic or forward dependency (e.g. s1 does not depend on s2 or s3).","evidence":"DEF.md:92 shows \"**Depends on:** `s1`\" (for s2) and DEF.md:107 shows \"**Depends on:** `s1`, `s2`\" (for s3). This exactly matches the premise: s2 depends on s1 alone, and s3 depends on both s1 and s2. No entry for s1 lists a dependency on s2 or s3, consistent with the no-cyclic/no-forward-dependency claim.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"cl10","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The functional-requirement ids use the pattern E20261030e6ddcdcb:S00N:FR00M where S001, S002, S003 correspond respectively to stories s1, s2, s3, and the hash suffix e6ddcdcb3d9cf330 matches the epic hash used in the Story ids (STORY-e6ddcdcb3d9cf330-sN) and the artifact id (DEF-e6ddcdcb3d9cf330).","evidence":"docs/epics/.../DEF.md:30-41 show FR ids E20261030e6ddcdcb:S001:FR001/FR002, S002:FR001/FR002, S003:FR001/FR002 - S001/S002/S003 map to stories s1/s2/s3 per headings at lines 69, 86, 101 (STORY-e6ddcdcb3d9cf330-s1/s2/s3). Line 1 confirms the artifact id DEF-e6ddcdcb3d9cf330 uses the same hash suffix e6ddcdcb3d9cf330 as the Story ids. All three parts of the premise are corroborated by the gathered matches.","action":"none - verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"cl11","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"LOW","premise":"The non-goal 'Build a replacement session-threading framework or assume another branch is already merged' presupposes an actual, separately developed session-thread lifecycle contract (referenced again in k7 and s1/ac6 and s3/ac2) that this epic must integrate with rather than reimplement.","evidence":"DEF.md:46 states the non-goal verbatim: 'Build a replacement session-threading framework or assume another branch is already merged... this epic integrates with its actual lifecycle contract.' DEF.md:64 (k7) consistently requires the epic to 'integrate the separately developed session-thread lifecycle' and explicitly forbids reintroducing a retired engine as a prerequisite. DEF.md:112 (an ac2) reiterates that 'execution integrates with the actual session-thread lifecycle rather than reimplementing it.' grep for 'SessionThread' (likely a concrete